  1023	/// 0.8.20 Slice 25 (R-20-SUR) — the **TC-11 pin's static migration guard**, and
  1024	/// the sibling of [`check_migration_accretion`].
  1025	///
  1026	/// # What it enforces
  1027	///
  1028	/// TC-11 pin A (HITL-ratified 2026-07-12; `dev/plans/plan-0.8.20.md` §2.1) rules
  1029	/// that anonymous / doc-seeded nodes stay `h:<content-hash>` **permanently** —
  1030	/// the anonymous-surrogate leg is CANCELLED, not deferred — and that enforcement
  1031	/// is "**no new column**": the record IS `canonical_nodes.logical_id`'s
  1032	/// null-ness. The invariant is therefore a PROHIBITION:
  1033	///
  1034	/// > No migration, backfill, or verb shall ever populate `logical_id` on an
  1035	/// > existing canonical row, and a stored row's id-space is NEVER re-derived.
  1036	///
  1037	/// This function is the migration half of that prohibition, checked STATICALLY —
  1038	/// before the SQL can ever run. Supplying a `logical_id` at WRITE time is what
  1039	/// makes a record governed; a migration is not a write time.
  1040	///
  1041	/// # Rejected shapes
  1042	///
  1043	/// On `canonical_nodes` / `canonical_edges` only:
  1044	///
  1045	/// - `UPDATE … SET … logical_id …` — the direct forward-mint;
  1046	/// - `INSERT`/`REPLACE … INTO …` that names `logical_id`, i.e. a recreate-and-copy
  1047	///   backfill (`SELECT COALESCE(logical_id, mint(…))`);
  1048	/// - `INSERT`/`REPLACE … INTO …` with NO explicit column list — it writes every
  1049	///   column, `logical_id` among them, without naming it;
  1050	/// - `ALTER TABLE … ADD COLUMN logical_id … DEFAULT …` — a default populates
  1051	///   every EXISTING row in one statement;
  1052	/// - `ALTER TABLE … RENAME … logical_id` — a rename turns an already-populated
  1053	///   column INTO the identity column with no write at all;
  1054	/// - `CREATE TRIGGER … logical_id …` — a deferred `UPDATE`;
  1055	/// - `CREATE TABLE …(… logical_id … DEFAULT …)` — same reasoning as `ADD COLUMN`;
  1056	/// - ANY of the write shapes above behind a leading `WITH … AS (…)` /
  1057	///   `WITH RECURSIVE …` CTE clause, which SQLite allows in FRONT of `UPDATE`,
  1058	///   `INSERT` and `DELETE` — see the keyword-independent catch-all below.
  1059	///
  1060	/// # Accepted shapes (the ladder already ships all four)
  1061	///
  1062	/// - DECLARING the column (`ALTER TABLE canonical_nodes ADD COLUMN logical_id
  1063	///   TEXT`, step 12) — declaration is not population;
  1064	/// - INDEXING it (`CREATE UNIQUE INDEX … ON canonical_nodes(logical_id) WHERE
  1065	///   superseded_at IS NULL`, steps 12/23);
  1066	/// - READING it as a PREDICATE (`WHERE source_id IS NULL AND logical_id IS
  1067	///   NULL`, step 21 — which writes only `source_id`);
  1068	/// - RE-DECLARING it in a `CREATE TABLE` recreate (step 23's TC-33 edge
  1069	///   recreate, which deliberately copies no rows).
  1070	///
  1071	/// # Deliberately conservative
  1072	///
  1073	/// The guard refuses any statement that WRITES INTO `logical_id` on a canonical
  1074	/// table, including a copy that would merely PRESERVE the value through a
  1075	/// table-recreate. Distinguishing "preserving copy" from "minting copy" needs
  1076	/// positional matching of an insert column list against a `SELECT` expression
  1077	/// list — fragile, and trivially gamed by the exact class of change the pin
  1078	/// exists to stop. A future recreate must follow step 23's shipped precedent
  1079	/// (declare the column; do not copy rows through it), or come back through the
  1080	/// HITL to change the pin itself.
  1081	///
  1082	/// Conservatism also decides the SCHEMA-QUALIFIED case: `main.canonical_nodes`,
  1083	/// `temp.canonical_nodes` and every quoted spelling of either half normalise to
  1084	/// the bare table name before the comparison, so qualifying the table is not an
  1085	/// escape. `main.canonical_nodes_old` is NOT the pinned table — the qualifier is
  1086	/// stripped, never widened into a substring match.
  1087	///
  1088	/// SQLite's `UPDATE OR <conflict-action> …` prefix is not an escape either. All
  1089	/// five actions (`ROLLBACK` / `ABORT` / `FAIL` / `IGNORE` / `REPLACE`, verified
  1090	/// against SQLite 3.45.1) sit BETWEEN the keyword and the table name, so the
  1091	/// clause is skipped — token-exactly, so a table genuinely named `or_ledger`
  1092	/// still reads as the table — before the comparison. The `INSERT`/`REPLACE` arm
  1093	/// anchors on ` INTO `, which the conflict clause precedes, so it never had the
  1094	/// hole.
  1095	///
  1096	/// The reverse error — over-rejection — is a real cost too, so table names are
  1097	/// matched by TOKEN, never by substring, on EVERY arm including the trigger one.
  1098	/// A `CREATE TRIGGER … ON canonical_nodes_backup` targets a different table and
  1099	/// is accepted; a trigger whose BODY writes `logical_id` on a real canonical
  1100	/// table is still rejected, because the trigger arm scans the whole fragment
  1101	/// (which, by the `;` split, carries the head AND the first body statement) for
  1102	/// a pinned table token, and later body statements are their own fragments.
  1103	///
  1104	/// Conservatism is also why the arms above are not the whole guard. Each of them
  1105	/// anchors on the statement's LEADING keyword, and SQLite's grammar puts an
  1106	/// optional `WITH …` CTE clause in front of `UPDATE` / `INSERT` / `DELETE`, so
  1107	/// `WITH x AS (SELECT 1) UPDATE canonical_nodes SET logical_id = …` (valid, and
  1108	/// verified running against SQLite 3.45.1) would match no arm at all. Rather
  1109	/// than teach the guard to skip balanced parentheses — a SQL parser by another
  1110	/// name — a keyword-INDEPENDENT catch-all runs FIRST and refuses any statement
  1111	/// that writes `logical_id` in an ASSIGNMENT position on a pinned table:
  1112	/// a ` SET ` clause naming the column while some token names a canonical table,
  1113	/// or an ` INTO ` target that IS a canonical table (naming the column, or
  1114	/// omitting the column list). Read positions — a `WHERE` predicate, an index
  1115	/// column list, a column declaration — are untouched, which is precisely what
  1116	/// keeps the four shipped accepts green. See
  1117	/// [`writes_pinned_identity_anywhere`], whose assignment half deliberately
  1118	/// over-rejects: after an arbitrary prefix the update TARGET cannot be located
  1119	/// without parsing, so merely MENTIONING a canonical table anywhere in a
  1120	/// statement that assigns `logical_id` is refused.
  1121	///
  1122	/// # No exemption escape hatch — deliberately
  1123	///
  1124	/// [`check_migration_accretion`] honours a `-- MIGRATION-ACCRETION-EXEMPTION: `
  1125	/// marker because accretion is a BUDGET an author may knowingly spend. The TC-11
  1126	/// pin is not a budget; it is TERMINAL-FOREVER. An escape hatch would defeat it,
  1127	/// so there is none: a marked offender is rejected identically to an unmarked
  1128	/// one. Changing the pin is an HITL decision, not a comment.
  1129	///
  1130	/// # Known limits (stated, not hidden)
  1131	///
  1132	/// This is a lexical guard, not a SQL parser. It normalises away comments and
  1133	/// string literals, unwraps `"…"` / `[…]` / `` `…` `` quoted identifiers, and
  1134	/// tightens whitespace around `.` so a schema qualifier is one token (SQLite
  1135	/// accepts `main . canonical_nodes`; verified against SQLite 3.45) — then it
  1136	/// splits on `;`. A `CREATE TRIGGER` body therefore splits into fragments: the
  1137	/// FIRST body statement stays glued to the `CREATE TRIGGER …` head (the split
  1138	/// point is the `;` that ends it) and is judged with the head by the trigger
  1139	/// arm's whole-fragment token scan; every LATER body statement is its own
  1140	/// fragment and is judged independently by the `UPDATE` / `INSERT` arms. It
  1141	/// cannot see through dynamically-built SQL, which migrations do not use
  1142	/// (`MIGRATIONS` holds `&'static str` literals).
  1143	///
  1144	/// A leading `WITH …` CTE is NOT among the limits — the keyword-independent
  1145	/// catch-all above closes that family. What remains open, stated plainly rather
  1146	/// than papered over, is **cross-statement table identity**: the guard judges
  1147	/// each `;`-separated statement ALONE and holds no model of which name refers to
  1148	/// which table over the course of a step. A migration that renames the pinned
  1149	/// table out of the way, writes `logical_id` under the new name, and renames it
  1150	/// back —
  1151	///
  1152	/// ```sql
  1153	/// ALTER TABLE canonical_nodes RENAME TO tmp_x;
  1154	/// UPDATE tmp_x SET logical_id = 'minted' WHERE logical_id IS NULL;
  1155	/// ALTER TABLE tmp_x RENAME TO canonical_nodes;
  1156	/// ```
  1157	///
  1158	/// — is therefore ACCEPTED, as is its sibling (`CREATE TABLE … AS SELECT …
  1159	/// 'minted' AS logical_id FROM canonical_nodes`, `DROP`, `RENAME TO
  1160	/// canonical_nodes`). Closing it means tracking table identity across
