  1343	/// The keyword-INDEPENDENT catch-all: does `statement` write `logical_id` on a
  1344	/// pinned table ANYWHERE, whatever it starts with?
  1345	///
  1346	/// Every other arm anchors on the leading keyword, so any prefix that displaces
  1347	/// it — SQLite's optional `WITH …` / `WITH RECURSIVE …` CTE clause is the one
  1348	/// that actually exists, and it is legal in front of `UPDATE`, `INSERT` and
  1349	/// `DELETE` — slips them all. Parsing CTE parentheses to find the real head
  1350	/// keyword would be a SQL parser; the pin is TERMINAL-FOREVER and its stated
  1351	/// stance is that over-rejection is a cost worth paying and under-rejection is
  1352	/// not, so this fires on WRITE POSITION instead of on statement shape:
  1353	///
  1354	/// - an assignment: the ` SET ` clause (up to `WHERE` / `FROM` / `RETURNING`)
  1355	///   names `logical_id`, and SOME token of the statement is a pinned table;
  1356	/// - an insert target: the table after ` INTO ` is pinned, and the statement
  1357	///   either names `logical_id` or omits the column list (which writes every
  1358	///   column, identity included) — the same rule the `INSERT` arm applies.
  1359	///
  1360	/// READ positions are deliberately untouched, which is exactly what keeps the
  1361	/// four shipped accepts green: a `WHERE … logical_id IS NULL` predicate (step
  1362	/// 21, which writes only `source_id`), an index column list (steps 12/23), a
  1363	/// bare `ADD COLUMN logical_id TEXT` declaration (step 12) and a `CREATE TABLE`
  1364	/// re-declaration (step 23) all name the column without assigning to it. The
  1365	/// insert half checks the TARGET table, not mere mention, so step 23's
  1366	/// `INSERT … INTO _fathomdb_open_state … SELECT … FROM canonical_edges` — which
  1367	/// reads a pinned table — stays accepted.
  1368	///
  1369	/// The assignment half is deliberately coarser than the `UPDATE` arm: it asks
  1370	/// only whether a pinned table is MENTIONED, because after an arbitrary prefix
  1371	/// the update target cannot be located token-wise without parsing. So
  1372	/// `WITH x AS (SELECT 1 FROM canonical_nodes) UPDATE other SET logical_id = …`
  1373	/// is refused too. That is over-rejection by design, and cheap to work around
  1374	/// honestly (don't name a canonical table in the CTE); the reverse mistake is
  1375	/// not recoverable.
  1376	fn writes_pinned_identity_anywhere(statement: &str, names_column: bool) -> bool {
  1377	    if names_column
  1378	        && mentions_pinned_table(statement)
  1379	        && set_clause(statement).is_some_and(|clause| clause.contains(PINNED_IDENTITY_COLUMN))
  1380	    {
  1381	        return true;
  1382	    }
  1383	    table_after_into(statement).is_some_and(|(table, rest)| {
  1384	        is_pinned_table(&table) && (names_column || !rest.trim_start().starts_with('('))
  1385	    })
  1386	}
